      "side": 1,
      "message": "The key\u0027s `source` is the source hash, which (ADR 0001 line 59) doesn\u0027t yet record what a body-level `cond-expand` found about libraries. So the cache serves a stale entry when that answer changes:\n\n```scheme\n;; lib/probe.sld\n(define-library (probe)\n  (import (scheme base))\n  (export which)\n  (begin\n    (cond-expand\n      ((library (opt feature)) (define which \u0027with-opt-feature))\n      (else (define which \u0027without)))))\n```\n\n- `vali --cache-dir C -I lib main.scm` prints `without` (cold).\n- `vali --cache-dir C -I lib -I extra main.scm`, where `extra/opt/feature.sld` exists, prints `without` (warm). Expected `with-opt-feature`, which `--no-cache` prints.\n- The reverse also happens: a cache filled with `-I extra` keeps saying `with-opt-feature` after `extra` is dropped.\n\nThe docs here (line 26) and ADR line 88 say the key covers \"what its `cond-expand`s found\", which is only true of declaration-level ones.\n\nEither record the body-level availability answers in the library\u0027s source hash (the loader already asks them before expansion, so they\u0027re known when the hash is made), or don\u0027t cache a library whose body tested `(library ...)` until that lands.",
      "revId": "b401f85c030810abf9c75a1534042d3d51ed55c7",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
